Let $S$ denote the desired sum. We will prove that $S=1.
ewline 	extbf{First solution:} 
ewline Write \[ \sum_{n=0}^\infty  rac{1}{k2^n+1} =  rac{1}{k+1} + \sum_{n=1}^\infty  rac{1}{k2^n+1}; \] then we may write $S = S_1+S_2$ where \[ S_1 = \sum_{k=1}^\infty  rac{(-1)^{k-1}}{k(k+1)} \] \[ S_2 = \sum_{k=1}^\infty  rac{(-1)^{k-1}}{k} \sum_{n=1}^\infty  rac{1}{k2^n+1}. \] The rearrangement is valid because both $S_1$ and $S_2$ converge absolutely in $k$, by comparison to $\sum 1/k^2$. To compute $S_1$, note that \[ \sum_{k=1}^N  rac{(-1)^{k-1}}{k(k+1)} = \sum_{k=1}^N  (-1)^{k-1}\left( rac{1}{k}- rac{1}{k+1} ight) = -1+ rac{(-1)^N}{N+1}+2\sum_{k=1}^N  rac{(-1)^{k-1}}{k} \] converges to $2\ln 2-1$ as $N	o\infty$, and so $S_1 = 2\ln 2-1$. To compute $S_2$, write $ rac{1}{k2^n+1} =  rac{1}{k2^n}\cdot  rac{1}{1+1/(k2^n)}$ as the geometric series $\sum_{m=0}^\infty  rac{(-1)^m}{k^{m+1} 2^{mn+n}}$, whence \[ S_2 = \sum_{k=1}^\infty \sum_{n=1}^\infty \sum_{m=0}^\infty  rac{(-1)^{k+m-1}}{k^{m+2} 2^{mn+n}}. \] (This step requires $n \geq 1$, as otherwise the geometric series would not converge for $k=0$.) Now note that this triple sum converges absolutely: we have \[ \sum_{m=0}^\infty  rac{1}{k^{m+2} 2^{mn+n}} =  rac{1}{k^2 2^n} \cdot  rac{1}{1- rac{1}{k 2^n}} =  rac{1}{k(k2^n-1)} \leq  rac{1}{k^2 2^{n-1}} \] and so \[ \sum_{k=1}^\infty \sum_{n=1}^\infty \sum_{m=0}^\infty  rac{1}{k^{m+2} 2^{mn+n}} \leq \sum_{k=1}^\infty \sum_{n=1}^\infty   rac{1}{k^2 2^{n-1}} = \sum_{k=1}^\infty  rac{2}{k^2} < \infty. \] Thus we can rearrange the sum to get \[ S_2 = \sum_{m=0}^\infty (-1)^m \left( \sum_{n=1}^\infty  rac{1}{2^{mn+n}}ight) \left(\sum_{k=1}^\infty  rac{(-1)^{k-1}}{k^{m+2}} ight). \] The sum in $n$ is the geometric series \[  rac{1}{2^{m+1}(1- rac{1}{2^{m+1}})} =  rac{1}{2^{m+1}-1}. \] If we write the sum in $k$ as $S_3$, then note that \[ \sum_{k=1}^\infty  rac{1}{k^{m+2}} = S_3 + 2 \sum_{k=1}^\infty  rac{1}{(2k)^{m+2}} = S_3 +  rac{1}{2^{m+1}} \sum_{k=1}^\infty  rac{1}{k^{m+2}} \] (where we can rearrange terms in the first equality because all of the series converge absolutely), and so \[ S_3 = \left(1- rac{1}{2^{m+1}}ight)  \sum_{k=1}^\infty  rac{1}{k^{m+2}}. \] It follows that \[ S_2 = \sum_{m=0}^\infty  rac{(-1)^m}{2^{m+1}} \sum_{k=1}^\infty  rac{1}{k^{m+2}} = \sum_{k=1}^\infty  rac{1}{2k^2} \sum_{m=0}^\infty \left(- rac{1}{2k}ight)^m = \sum_{k=1}^\infty  rac{1}{k(2k+1)} = 2 \sum_{k=1}^\infty \left(  rac{1}{2k} -  rac{1}{2k+1} ight) = 2(1-\ln 2). \] Finally, we have $S = S_1 + S_2 = 1$. 
ewline 	extbf{Second solution:} 
ewline (by Tewodros Amdeberhan) Since $\int_0^1 x^t\,dx =  rac{1}{1+t}$ for any $t \geq 1$, we also have \[ S = \sum_{k=1}^\infty  \sum_{n=0}^\infty  rac{(-1)^{k-1}}{k} \int_0^1 x^{k2^n}\,dx. \] Again by absolute convergence, we are free to permute the integral and the sums: \[ S = \int_0^1 dx\, \sum_{n=0}^\infty \sum_{k=1}^\infty  rac{(-1)^{k-1}}{k} x^{k2^n} = \int_0^1 dx\, \sum_{n=0}^\infty \log (1 + x^{2^n}). \] Due to the uniqueness of binary expansions of nonnegative integers, we have the identity of formal power series \[  rac{1}{1 - x} = \prod_{n=0}^\infty (1 + x^{2^n}); \] the product converges absolutely for $0 \leq x < 1$. We thus have \[ S = -\int_0^1 \log (1-x)\,dx = \left((1-x) \log (1-x) - (1-x)ight)_0^1 = 1. \] 
ewline 	extbf{Third solution:} 
ewline (by Serin Hong) Again using absolute convergence, we may write \[ S = \sum_{m=2}^\infty  rac{1}{m} \sum_{k}  rac{(-1)^{k-1}}{k} \] where $k$ runs over all positive integers for which $m = k2^n+1$ for some $n$. If we write $e$ for the 2-adic valuation of $m-1$ and $j = (m-1)2^{-e}$ for the odd part of $m-1$, then the values of $k$ are $j 2^i$ for $i=0,\dots,e$. The inner sum can thus be evaluated as \[  rac{1}{j} - \sum_{i=1}^e  rac{1}{2^i j} =  rac{1}{2^e j} =  rac{1}{m-1}. \] We thus have \[ S = \sum_{m=2}^\infty  rac{1}{m(m-1)} = \sum_{m=2}^\infty \left(  rac{1}{m-1} -  rac{1}{m} ight) = 1. \] 
ewline 	extbf{Fourth solution:} 
ewline (by Liang Xiao) Let $S_0$ and $S_1$ be the sums $\sum_k  rac{1}{k} \sum_{n=0}^\infty  rac{1}{k2^n+1}$ with $k$ running over all odd and all even positive integers, respectively, so that \[ S = S_0 - S_1. \] In $S_1$, we may write $k = 2\ell$ to obtain \[ S_1 = \sum_{\ell=1}^\infty  rac{1}{2\ell} \sum_{n=0}^\infty  rac{1}{\ell 2^{n+1} + 1} =  rac{1}{2} (S_0 + S_1) - \sum_{\ell=1}^\infty  rac{1}{2\ell(\ell+1)} =  rac{1}{2} (S_0 + S_1) -  rac{1}{2} \] because the last sum telescopes; this immediately yields $S = 1$.

problem:
Let $n > 1$ be an integer. Find, with proof, all sequences $x_1 , x_2 , \ldots , x_{n-1}$ of positive integers with the following three properties: 
(a). $x_1 < x_2 < \cdots < x_{n-1}$ ; 
(b). $x_i + x_{n-i} = 2n$ for all $i = 1, 2, \ldots , n - 1$; 
(c). given any two indices $i$ and $j$ (not necessarily distinct) for which $x_i + x_j < 2n$, there is an index $k$ such that $x_i + x_j = x_k$.

answer:
\begin{cases}
n^2 - n + 1 & \text{if } n \text{ is odd}, \\
n^2 & \text{if } n \text{ is even}.
\end{cases}
